<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/expert/multivariate-statistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Multivariate statistics analyses <strong>many variables simultaneously</strong> — because real
    things have many attributes at once, and one-variable-at-a-time analysis both misses
    interactions and drowns in repeated tests. It is also the mathematical home of modern machine
    learning: regression with many predictors, classification, and dimension reduction are all
    multivariate statistics under different marketing names.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four ideas that organise high-dimensional data" widget="equationStep" config={multivariateCfg} />

  <h2>The methods and where they live</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multiple regression & GLMs:</strong> many predictors, one outcome — the general linear model family (including logistic regression for yes/no outcomes) is applied statistics' backbone.</li>
    <li><strong>PCA & factor analysis:</strong> compress correlated variables into a few components. Face recognition's "eigenfaces" and market-research's brand dimensions are both PCA wearing different clothes.</li>
    <li><strong>MANOVA & mixed models:</strong> compare groups across several outcomes at once; handle repeated measures and hierarchical data (students in classes in schools) with mixed effects — the standard tool of modern clinical and education research.</li>
    <li><strong>The multiple-testing burden grows with dimension:</strong> test 10,000 genes and thousands of "significant" results appear by chance. False discovery rate (Benjamini–Hochberg, 1995) is the standard control.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Johnson & Wichern, Applied Multivariate Statistical Analysis; Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, ESL.</p>
</TopicPage>
